body:
Hi team, I was running some transaction workloads (sysbench and TPC-C) against DuckDB, and found that every statement spends a noticeable part of its optimization time constructing the expression rewrite rules, although they are identical for every statement.

Every statement creates a new `Optimizer` ([code ref 1](https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/blob/392cee07532cad39193661814ff35309b7437b1f/src/main/client_context.cpp#L507)), whose `ExpressionRewriter` ([code ref 2](https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/blob/392cee07532cad39193661814ff35309b7437b1f/src/include/duckdb/optimizer/optimizer.hpp#L44)) allocates and then destroys all 39 default rules and their matcher trees ([code ref 3](https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/blob/392cee07532cad39193661814ff35309b7437b1f/src/optimizer/expression_rewriter.cpp#L22-L73)), even on every `Execute` of a prepared statement, which costs 6.6% of the client thread in a sysbench point select. The rules hold no per-statement state and use their rewriter only to reach the client context and the static `ConstantOrNull` ([code ref 4](https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/blob/392cee07532cad39193661814ff35309b7437b1f/src/include/duckdb/optimizer/rule.hpp#L17-L31)), so one set per client can be shared.

## Benchmark setup

| Workload | Data | Transaction | Used for |
|---|---|---|---|
| sysbench `point_select` | `sbtest1`, 1M rows | `SELECT c FROM sbtest1 WHERE id = $1` | profile and benchmark |
| sysbench `read_only` | `sbtest1`, 1M rows | `BEGIN`; 10 point selects; 1 each of `BETWEEN` range, `SUM` range, `ORDER BY` range, `DISTINCT` range (100 rows each); `COMMIT` | benchmark |
| TPC-C | 16 warehouses | 45% NewOrder, 43% Payment, 4% each of OrderStatus, Delivery and StockLevel | benchmark |

- Machine: Apple M5 Pro (18 cores), macOS 26.5. `reldebug` build of `392cee07532` (main) vs. this branch.
- The driver below runs on the DuckDB C++ API. Every client thread opens its own `Connection`, prepares all statements once, and runs transactions in a closed loop, binding parameters per execution. DuckDB keeps its default thread count.
- 1 and 8 client threads, 3 s warm-up, then 8 s measured.
- Persistent database, restored from a pristine copy before every run.
- Baseline and patched runs alternate on every configuration, and the order flips every repetition so that drift on the machine hits both sides equally. The tables below report the median of 3 runs.

<details>
<summary>Benchmark driver (click to expand)</summary>

```cpp
// The DuckDB backend of the driver; the workloads call Prepare once per statement and Run per execution.
struct DuckConn : Conn {
	duckdb::Connection con;
	map<int, unique_ptr<duckdb::PreparedStatement>> stmts;
	map<int, vector<duckdb::LogicalType>> types;
	unique_ptr<duckdb::PreparedStatement> begin, commit, rollback;
	explicit DuckConn(duckdb::DuckDB &db) : con(db) {
		begin = con.Prepare("BEGIN TRANSACTION");
		commit = con.Prepare("COMMIT");
		rollback = con.Prepare("ROLLBACK");
	}
	void Prepare(int id, const string &sql) override {
		auto st = con.Prepare(sql);
		if (st->HasError()) {
			throw runtime_error("prepare failed: " + sql + "\n" + st->GetError());
		}
		// parameters are cast to the expected types before every Execute
		auto expected = st->GetExpectedParameterTypes();
		vector<duckdb::LogicalType> t(st->GetParameterCount(), duckdb::LogicalType(duckdb::LogicalTypeId::UNKNOWN));
		for (auto &e : expected) {
			t[std::stoul(e.first) - 1] = e.second;
		}
		types[id] = t;
		stmts[id] = std::move(st);
	}
	unique_ptr<Res> Run(duckdb::PreparedStatement &st, duckdb::vector<duckdb::Value> &vals) {
		duckdb::QueryParameters qp;
		auto r = st.Execute(vals, qp);
		if (r->HasError()) {
			throw DbError(r->GetError());
		}
		auto res = make_unique<DuckRes>();
		while (auto ch = r->Fetch()) {
			if (ch->size() == 0) {
				break;
			}
			res->n += ch->size();
			res->chunks.push_back(std::move(ch));
		}
		return std::move(res);
	}
};

// sysbench: sbtest1(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, k INTEGER, c CHAR(120), pad CHAR(60)), INDEX k_1(k)
c.Prepare(POINT, "SELECT c FROM sbtest1 WHERE id = $1");
c.Prepare(SIMPLE_RANGE, "SELECT c FROM sbtest1 WHERE id BETWEEN $1 AND $2");
c.Prepare(SUM_RANGE, "SELECT SUM(k) FROM sbtest1 WHERE id BETWEEN $1 AND $2");
c.Prepare(ORDER_RANGE, "SELECT c FROM sbtest1 WHERE id BETWEEN $1 AND $2 ORDER BY c");
c.Prepare(DISTINCT_RANGE, "SELECT DISTINCT c FROM sbtest1 WHERE id BETWEEN $1 AND $2 ORDER BY c");
```

</details>

