## Problem

Since #26676, the first deleting transaction in a vector is kept in a bitmask (`MASKED` state). But when a vector already holds a committed delete in its bitmask and **another transaction deletes more rows**, `AssignId` has only one mask and cannot represent a third value (mask id / new id / not deleted). It falls back to `SetIds` -> `GetInitializedDeletedPointer` -> `DecompressDeleteMask`, which allocates the full `transaction_t[STANDARD_VECTOR_SIZE]` array (16 KB) from the `FixedSizeAllocator`.

This memory is not evictable and stays pinned until `CHECKPOINT` (`CompressVersionIds`). Workloads that repeatedly rewrite the same rows pin ~8 bytes per row of every touched vector, and inside a large transaction this fills `memory_limit` with unspillable memory.

## Solution

The `MASKED` state can now carry an optional **second bitmask**:

- `base_mask` / `base_delete_id`: rows holding an earlier *committed* delete
- `deleted_mask` / `mask_delete_id`: rows deleted by the current transaction
- all other rows: `constant_delete_id` (`NOT_DELETED_ID`)

The two masks never overlap: a transaction never writes a row that holds a committed delete (the conflict check in `WriteRows` throws before). So a second delete costs ~256 bytes per vector instead of 16 KB.

### Demote the committed delete into the base mask (`AssignId`)

```cpp
if (IsDeleteId(id) && !IsCommitted(id) && !HasBaseMask() && IsDeleteId(mask_delete_id) &&
    IsCommitted(mask_delete_id) && constant_delete_id == NOT_DELETED_ID) {
	// keep the committed delete in the base mask and the new rows in the mask,
	// instead of materializing the 16 KB per-row array
	base_mask.Initialize(deleted_mask);
	base_delete_id = mask_delete_id;
	deleted_mask.Reset();
	deleted_mask.Initialize(STANDARD_VECTOR_SIZE);
	deleted_mask.SetAllInvalid(STANDARD_VECTOR_SIZE);
	mask_delete_id = id;
	for (idx_t i = 0; i < count; i++) {
		deleted_mask.SetValidUnsafe(rows ? rows[i] : i);
	}
	return;
}
```

### Readers know about the second mask

```cpp
transaction_t ChunkVectorInfo::GetDeleteId(idx_t row) const {
	D_ASSERT(delete_state != DeleteIdState::ARRAY);
	if (delete_state == DeleteIdState::MASKED) {
		if (deleted_mask.RowIsValid(row)) {
			return mask_delete_id;
		}
		if (HasBaseMask() && base_mask.RowIsValid(row)) {
			return base_delete_id;
		}
	}
	return constant_delete_id;
}
```

`TemplatedGetSelVector` uses a per-row path only when a base mask exists (the single-mask scan is unchanged). `HasDeletes`, `ToString` and `DecompressDeleteMask` are updated too.

### Rollback

If all rows of the transaction are reverted, the base mask becomes the only mask again (`RestoreBaseMask`). Before, the committed delete would have been lost.

### Merge once both deletes are visible to all (`CompressVersionIds`)

```cpp
if (delete_state == DeleteIdState::MASKED && HasBaseMask() && IsDeleteId(mask_delete_id) &&
    IsCommitted(mask_delete_id)) {
	if (mask_delete_id < lowest_visibility_bound && base_delete_id < lowest_visibility_bound) {
		MergeBaseMask(); // union of both masks, id 0; collapses to a constant if every row is deleted
	} else {
		pending = true; // retry when the lowest active start advances
	}
}
```

Vectors with a base mask stay "pending" until merged, so the cached compression state (`recheck_compression`) stays armed and `VerifyCachedCompressionState` asserts `!HasBaseMask()` for settled vectors.

Update markers on a vector with a base mask still fall back to the per-row array, so that path is unchanged.

## Limitation

Two committed deletes can be held per vector. A third transaction touching the same vector *before* the masks are merged (merging only happens in `CompressVersionIds` at checkpoint) still falls back to the per-row array. Merging at commit time could cover that as a follow-up.

## Tests

New test `test/sql/storage/delete_second_transaction_mask.test`, based on the reproduction in the issue (600 vectors, one delete per vector, then a second delete in the same vectors). It checks:

- `BASE_TABLE` memory growth stays < 2 MB inside the transaction, after commit, and after a delete into a settled vector (the old behavior pins ~9.8 MB)
- an older snapshot still sees all rows, newer ones see both deletes
- rollback restores the two committed deletes
- a write-write conflict on the same row still raises `Conflict on tuple deletion`
- both deletes are merged after the old snapshot ends and a checkpoint runs
- results are correct after a restart

## Problem

Several catalog commands reject nested schema paths or use only the last schema name. This can list tables from the wrong schema, bind a foreign key to the wrong table, or produce misleading errors. Data generators can also load or replace tables in the wrong schema. `USE`, `schema` and `search_path` also reject nested schemas.

The SQL below sets up `lake` with a nested `s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail` schema and a top-level `retail` schema. We use this setup in the examples for each issue and its fix. Each fix has its own commit.

```sql
ATTACH ':memory:' AS lake;
USE lake;
CREATE SCHEMA lake.s3tablescatalog;
CREATE SCHEMA lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo;
CREATE SCHEMA lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail;
CREATE SCHEMA lake.retail;
CREATE TABLE lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.widget_sales AS SELECT 42 AS id;
CREATE TABLE lake.retail.other AS SELECT 7 AS id;
```

## SHOW TABLES

The first two commands previously failed in the parser. The third included `widget_sales` from the nested schema. They now resolve the full path and filter by schema identity.

```sql
SHOW TABLES FROM lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail; -- widget_sales
SHOW TABLES FROM s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail;      -- widget_sales
SHOW TABLES FROM lake.retail;                          -- only other
```

Prepared `SHOW TABLES` statements also follow later `USE` changes. `PRAGMA show_tables` now goes through the same binder, including when prepared through the C++ API.

```sql
PREPARE list_tables AS SHOW TABLES;
USE lake.retail;
EXECUTE list_tables; -- only other
USE lake;
```

The same behavior applies to an API-prepared pragma:

```cpp
con.Query("USE lake");
auto tables = con.Prepare("PRAGMA show_tables");
con.Query("USE lake.retail");
auto result = tables->Execute(); // only other
con.Query("USE lake");
```

Schema IDs are resolved again when a prepared statement runs. Dropping and recreating the schema no longer leaves `SHOW TABLES FROM` with a stale ID.

```sql
CREATE SCHEMA lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.recreated;
PREPARE list_recreated AS SHOW TABLES FROM lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.recreated;
DROP SCHEMA lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.recreated;
CREATE SCHEMA lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.recreated;
CREATE TABLE lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.recreated.t(i INTEGER);
EXECUTE list_recreated; -- t
DROP SCHEMA lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.recreated CASCADE;
```

`SHOW ALL TABLES` also merged same-named objects from schemas with the same leaf name. It now groups tables and views by their object IDs.

```sql
CREATE TABLE lake.retail.widget_sales(id INTEGER, v VARCHAR);
SHOW ALL TABLES;
-- Two widget_sales rows, with [id] and [id, v] as their column lists.
```

## DROP SEQUENCE and DROP INDEX

Creation already worked, but the corresponding `DROP` commands rejected the longer names. Both pairs now succeed. The parser generator renumbers its shared tables when the index rule changes, which accounts for most of the generated diff.

```sql
CREATE SEQUENCE lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.seq;
DROP SEQUENCE lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.seq;

CREATE INDEX idx ON lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.widget_sales(id);
DROP INDEX lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.idx;
```

## USE, schema and search_path

These commands previously rejected nested schemas. They now retain the full path, so unqualified reads and creates use the selected schema. `SHOW SCHEMAS` marks that schema as current, including in prepared statements. The change reuses the existing search-path representation.

```sql
USE lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail;
SELECT * FROM widget_sales; -- 42
SHOW TABLES; -- only widget_sales
SELECT database_name, schema_name FROM (SHOW SCHEMAS) WHERE current;
-- lake, retail: exactly one current row, for the selected nested schema.

USE s3tablescatalog.salesdemo;
SELECT current_schema(); -- s3tablescatalog.salesdemo

SET schema = 'lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail';
SELECT * FROM widget_sales; -- 42
CREATE TABLE created_here AS SELECT 99 AS id;
SELECT * FROM lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.created_here; -- 99

SET search_path = 'lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail';
SELECT * FROM widget_sales; -- 42

USE lake; -- Reset the default for the following examples.
```

A schema found through another catalog's search-path entry now retains that catalog. Previously, `USE` could succeed but store the schema under the default catalog, where it did not exist.

```sql
ATTACH ':memory:' AS other;
SET search_path = 'other.main,lake.s3tablescatalog';
USE s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail;
CREATE TABLE resolved_here AS SELECT 123 AS id;
SELECT * FROM lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.resolved_here; -- 123
USE lake;
DETACH other;
```

Remote pushdown checks now use the full local schema path too. With a remote catalog also in the search path, the local lookup for `widget_sales` checks `lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail`. The previous check used `lake.retail`.

## dbgen and dsdgen

After `USE` selected a nested schema, `dbgen` and `dsdgen` still targeted the top-level schema with the same last name. `dsdgen(overwrite=true)` could replace an existing table there. Both generators now carry the full schema path through binding, table creation, and data loading.

```sql
LOAD tpch;
LOAD tpcds;
CREATE TABLE lake.retail.customer_address AS SELECT 42 AS sentinel;
USE lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail;
CALL dbgen(sf=0.01, suffix='_tpch');
CALL dsdgen(sf=0, overwrite=true);
SELECT sentinel FROM lake.retail.customer_address; -- still 42
SELECT count(*) FROM lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.nation_tpch; -- 25
SELECT count(*) FROM lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.customer_address; -- 0
USE lake;
```

`schema=` follows core's string-schema rules. It prefers an existing nested path and falls back to a literal name. Explicit double quotes select the literal name. Resolution uses the final `catalog=` value, regardless of parameter order.

```sql
CREATE SCHEMA lake.retail.reporting;
CREATE SCHEMA lake."retail.reporting";
CALL dbgen(sf=0, schema='retail.reporting');   -- lake.retail.reporting
CALL dbgen(sf=0, schema='"retail.reporting"'); -- lake."retail.reporting"
```

## Foreign keys

A reference could silently bind to a different table when both schemas ended in `retail`. These commands now report the existing cross-schema error in both directions.

```sql
CREATE TABLE lake.retail.pk(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY);
CREATE TABLE lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.pk(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY);

CREATE TABLE lake.retail.fk(
    id INTEGER REFERENCES lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.pk(id)
);
CREATE TABLE lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.fk(
    id INTEGER REFERENCES lake.retail.pk(id)
);
```

Valid references within the same schema retain their full path in the saved constraint SQL, including after reopening the database.

```sql
CREATE TABLE lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.fk(
    id INTEGER REFERENCES s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.pk(id)
);
-- Saved constraint: REFERENCES s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.pk(id)
```

## Schemas under main

The lookup for `main.sub.t` previously selected `temp.main` and failed. It now resolves against the default database.

```sql
CREATE SCHEMA lake.main.sub;
CREATE TABLE lake.main.sub.t AS SELECT 7 AS i;
SELECT * FROM main.sub.t; -- 7, as with lake.main.sub.t
```

## Missing-entry errors and suggestions

A missing nested function previously reported that the first schema component was a missing catalog. The error now identifies the missing function. Suggestions also include the full nested path.

```sql
SELECT s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.nope(1);
-- Scalar Function with name nope does not exist.

CREATE TABLE lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.example_table(id INTEGER);
SELECT * FROM example_tabl;
-- Did you mean "lake.s3tablescatalog.salesdemo.retail.example_table"?
```

## Catalogs without nested-schema support

An extension that discards parent schema names can read or modify the wrong object. Core now rejects nested paths when the catalog reports `SupportsNestedSchemas() == false`. The check covers entry lookup, schema-path lookup, schema creation and dropping, and remote pushdown before it bypasses normal binding.

For an attached catalog `ext` without nested-schema support, these statements fail with a clear unsupported-schema error:

```sql
SELECT * FROM ext.a.b.t;
CREATE TABLE ext.a.b.t3(i INTEGER);
USE ext.a.b;
CREATE SCHEMA ext.a.b;
DROP SCHEMA ext.a.b CASCADE;
```

The error includes the rejected schema path. If `ext` is the default catalog, a misspelled catalog name such as `lakee` is interpreted as a parent schema. The message now shows that interpretation:

```sql
USE ext;
SELECT * FROM lakee.sales.t;
-- Catalog "ext" does not support nested schemas: lakee.sales
```

`ext."a.b".t` still names one literal schema, so the guard does not reject it. Quack now declares the nested-schema support it already implements. DuckLake already declares support for 1.1 metadata; its 1.0 test accepts the earlier core error.

## Validation

Built with `FORCE_DEBUG=1 FORCE_ASSERT=1` after merging `main`. The focused run passed 42 test cases with 1,890 assertions. One parser test was skipped because autocomplete is unavailable. Coverage includes nested catalog commands, search paths, prepared SHOW statements, unsupported extension catalogs, Quack nested-schema lookup and DDL/DML, and DuckLake metadata 1.0 and 1.1 behavior.

The error-message follow-up passed the catalog capability regression and DuckLake version gate with 185 assertions. The checks cover mistyped catalog names, full schema paths, and identifier quoting through entry lookup, schema lookup, CREATE/DROP SCHEMA, and remote pushdown.

Nine focused TPCH/TPCDS tests passed with 263 assertions. They cover both generation modes, overwrite protection, named-schema resolution and quoting, catalog-parameter order, read-only catalogs, and existing generation behavior. The new regressions reproduced the failures before the fixes. Clang-tidy passed on the changed binding lines.

The parser trampoline was regenerated from the merged grammar. Formatting and extension patch checks passed. Full-suite validation is left to CI.

Partial aggregate pushdown can give a DECIMAL sum 100x too big.

A filter over both join sides makes FilterStatisticsOptimizer run stats propagation before the pushdown, which swaps sum(DECIMAL) for sum_no_overflow on the raw ints. Pushdown exports that state, finalize returns HUGEINT, and the CAST to DECIMAL(38,2) scales it again.

    CREATE TABLE fact AS SELECT (i % 100)::BIGINT AS k, (1.25 + (i % 3))::DECIMAL(7,2) AS price FROM range(20000) t(i);
    CREATE TABLE dim AS SELECT (i % 100)::BIGINT AS k, (i % 5)::BIGINT AS attr FROM range(2000) t(i);
    CREATE TABLE other AS SELECT i::BIGINT AS attr, i::BIGINT AS x FROM range(5) t(i);
    SELECT a.attr, a.s
    FROM (SELECT d.attr, sum(f.price) AS s FROM fact f JOIN dim d ON f.k = d.k GROUP BY d.attr) a
    LEFT JOIN other o ON a.attr = o.attr
    WHERE a.s > 0 OR o.x > 0;
    -- 17998000.00 instead of 179980.00

Fix: if the implementation was swapped, rebind the aggregate from its definition before export, else skip the pushdown. Test added, fails on main.

