`CREATE VIEW` drops the view's own catalog from the references in its query, so the view keeps working when the database is attached under a different alias. `StripCatalogName(TableRef &)` had no case for `PivotRef`, so references inside a `PIVOT`/`UNPIVOT` kept the qualifier:

```sql
ATTACH 'a.db' AS db1;
CREATE TABLE db1.t(x INTEGER);
CREATE VIEW db1.v AS PIVOT db1.t ON x IN (42) USING count(*);
-- stored as: SELECT * FROM db1.t PIVOT (count_star() FOR (x) IN (42))
```

Re-attaching the file as `db2` then broke the view, or made it read a table of whichever database is attached as `db1`.

This strips the pivot source, the aggregates, the pivot expressions and the unpivot entries.

Follow-up to #27890. A member drop committed after an in-place storage upgrade makes the database fail to attach with default options:

```sql
ATTACH 'u.db' AS u (STORAGE_VERSION 'v2.0.0');
CREATE TABLE u.t(i INTEGER);
CHECKPOINT u;
DETACH u;

-- upgrades the database in place, the file header still says v2.0.0 until the next checkpoint
ATTACH 'u.db' AS u (STORAGE_VERSION 'v2.1.0');
CREATE MACRO u.f(x INTEGER) AS x + 1, (x VARCHAR) AS 42;
DROP MACRO u.f(VARCHAR);
DETACH u; -- with the drop still in the WAL

ATTACH 'u.db' AS u;
-- Catalog Error: Failure while replaying WAL file "u.db.wal": Dropping a single overload is only
-- supported for storage versions v2.1.0 and higher.
```

No data is lost: attaching with `STORAGE_VERSION 'v2.1.0'` replays the WAL, and after a checkpoint the file opens normally. A default attach should still replay any committed record.

The storage-version gate for member drops sits in `MacroCatalogEntry::AlterEntry`. WAL replay applies the `ALTER_MACRO` record through the same `AlterEntry`, and at that point the storage manager reports the version from the file header (v2.0.0). The gate then rejects a record that passed it when it was committed. The `RETURNS` gate in `bind_create.cpp` does not have this problem because replay does not bind.

The fix skips the gate while the storage manager is still loading (`StorageManager::IsLoaded()`). This flag is false during checkpoint load and WAL replay and true for every statement after `ATTACH` completes. `CheckTypeIsSupported` in `duck_table_entry.cpp` already uses it the same way for the TUPLE column gate, so existing files stay attachable. The change stays in the macro entry. No generic layer gets a macro-specific branch. Moving the gate to bind time would have required a new hook on `AlterInfo` and the generic alter binding for one check, and replay already has the right signal.

Statement-time behavior is unchanged. A member drop on a v2.0.0 database is rejected with the same message, including on a database whose WAL was just replayed under a v2.0.0 header.

`drop_function_signature_upgrade_replay.test` covers the reproducer with three member drops replayed from the WAL, one of them `DROP MACRO IF EXISTS` on an existing signature. The three dropped overloads are rejected after replay and again after a checkpoint and reattach, and the surviving overload and the table data stay intact. On the replayed attach, whose header is still v2.0.0, a new member drop is rejected at statement time. A v2.0.0 database that was never upgraded rejects a member drop with the same message as before and keeps both overloads.
